---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const pageTitle = "How to Sign an APK for Google Play Store Publishing";
const pageDescription = "Learn how to sign your SiteToApp APK: generate a keystore, sign the release, and upload to Google Play Console. Walkthrough with commands.";

const breadcrumb = {
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://sitetoapp.dev/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Guides", "item": "https://sitetoapp.dev/guides" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "How to Sign an APK for Google Play Store Publishing" }
  ]
};

const signApkSchema = { "@graph": [{
  "@type": "HowTo",
  "name": "How to sign an APK for Google Play Store publishing",
  "description": "Sign the Android APK generated by SiteToApp so Google Play accepts it: create an upload keystore, build the release APK, sign with apksigner, and verify the signature.",
  "step": [
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Generate an upload keystore", "text": "Create a keystore with keytool (Android Studio or the JDK) and store it safely — you will need the same key for every update." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Build the release APK", "text": "In your SiteToApp workspace ZIP, open the Android project and run ./gradlew assembleRelease (or use Android Studio's Generate Signed Bundle/APK wizard)." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Sign with apksigner", "text": "Sign the release APK: apksigner sign --ks <keystore> app-release.apk. Google Play requires a signature from your upload key." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Verify the signature", "text": "Run apksigner verify --print-certs app-release.apk and confirm it verifies successfully before uploading." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Upload to Google Play Console", "text": "Add the signed APK as a new release in Play Console, complete the store listing, and submit for review. Follow the full checklist in the Play Store publishing guide." }
  ]
}, breadcrumb]};
---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} bodyClass="landing-page" structuredData={signApkSchema}>
  <main class="shell">
    <article class="content-panel">
      <h1>How to Sign an APK for Google Play Store Publishing</h1>
      <p>Every APK uploaded to Google Play must be cryptographically signed with your developer upload key. This guide walks through signing the APK that <a href="/android">SiteToApp</a> generates — from creating a keystore to verifying the signature — so your release is accepted by Play Console on the first attempt.</p>

      <h2>Why signing matters</h2>
      <p>An app signature proves the APK was built by you (or someone with access to your key). Google uses it to guarantee that updates to your app always come from the same trusted source. The debug APK you download and test locally is <em>not</em> accepted for publishing — you need a release-signed build.</p>
      <p><strong>Important:</strong> never lose your keystore file. If you lose it without Google's key recovery, you'll be unable to push updates to the same listing — you'd have to publish as a brand-new app.</p>

      <h2>Step 1 — Generate an upload keystore</h2>
      <p>The easiest route is Android Studio: <strong>File → Project Structure → Signing &amp; Configs → Create new keystore</strong>. Or run this from the command line (requires JDK or Android Studio's bundled JDK):</p>
      <pre>keytool -genkeypair -keystore upload-key.jks -alias upload -keyalg RSA -keysize 2048 -validity 10000</pre>
      <p>Fill in the prompts (name, organisation, country code) and choose a strong password. Store the resulting <code>upload-key.jks</code> file in at least two secure, separate locations — an encrypted cloud backup and a local copy are both good options. Never commit it to a public repository.</p>

      <h2>Step 2 — Build the release APK from the workspace</h2>
      <p>Unzip the SiteToApp workspace ZIP. In the project root, run:</p>
      <pre>./gradlew assembleRelease</pre>
      <p>The unsigned release APK lands in <code>app/build/outputs/apk/release/</code>. If you prefer a GUI, Android Studio's <em>Build → Generate Signed Bundle / APK</em> wizard handles steps 2 and 3 in one flow.</p>

      <h2>Step 3 — Sign with apksigner</h2>
      <p><code>apksigner</code> ships with the Android SDK build-tools. Run:</p>
      <pre>apksigner sign --ks upload-key.jks --ks-key-alias upload --out app-signed.apk app-release-unsigned.apk</pre>
      <p>You'll be prompted for the keystore password. The output file <code>app-signed.apk</code> is what you upload to Play Console.</p>

      <h2>Step 4 — Verify the signature</h2>
      <p>Before uploading, confirm the signing succeeded:</p>
      <pre>apksigner verify --print-certs app-signed.apk</pre>
      <p>If you see certificate details printed without any errors, the APK is correctly signed and ready for Play Console.</p>

      <h2>Step 5 — Upload to Play Console</h2>
      <p>In Play Console, go to <strong>Production → Releases → Create new release → Upload</strong> and add <code>app-signed.apk</code>. If Play Console flags the target API level, the SiteToApp template already targets a current API, so simply confirm and proceed. Write a release note and submit for review.</p>
      <p>Need the full publishing walkthrough from scratch? Follow our <a href="/guides/publish-to-play-store">step-by-step Play Store publishing guide</a>.</p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
